1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:03,996
In March 2023, a photo of the Pope

2
00:00:03,966 --> 00:00:06,538
in a white puffer jacket fooled millions of

3
00:00:06,508 --> 00:00:09,790
people. It was not real. Nobody had taken

4
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:12,528
it. A piece of software had imagined it.

5
00:00:12,895 --> 00:00:14,631
Three years later, that is the

6
00:00:14,601 --> 00:00:17,590
least impressive thing artificial intelligence can do.

7
00:00:18,177 --> 00:00:20,831
So what changed, how fast, and what

8
00:00:20,801 --> 00:00:23,584
is still coming? To make sense of it,

9
00:00:23,554 --> 00:00:26,960
we need one simple idea: the S-curve.

10
00:00:27,292 --> 00:00:31,002
Almost every technology grows in three phases.

11
00:00:30,972 --> 00:00:33,487
First comes a slow start: years of

12
00:00:33,457 --> 00:00:37,828
research, clumsy prototypes and very few users.

13
00:00:38,218 --> 00:00:40,303
Then comes the steep part, where

14
00:00:40,273 --> 00:00:42,729
everything improves at once and the world

15
00:00:42,699 --> 00:00:44,655
changes in just a few years.

16
00:00:44,981 --> 00:00:48,727
And finally the curve flattens. The technology

17
00:00:48,697 --> 00:00:50,991
matures, and each new version is

18
00:00:50,961 --> 00:00:52,800
only a little better than the last.

19
00:00:53,173 --> 00:00:55,456
Mobile phones are a clean example. For

20
00:00:55,426 --> 00:00:57,708
years they had tiny screens, no apps

21
00:00:57,678 --> 00:00:59,587
and a battery that died by lunch.

22
00:00:59,979 --> 00:01:02,170
Then the smartphone arrived, and within

23
00:01:02,140 --> 00:01:03,760
a decade it was in nearly

24
00:01:03,730 --> 00:01:06,535
every pocket. Today, a new model looks

25
00:01:06,505 --> 00:01:08,321
almost the same as last year's.

26
00:01:08,683 --> 00:01:12,097
Flight is another one. In 1903, the Wright

27
00:01:12,067 --> 00:01:14,790
brothers stayed in the air for twelve seconds.

28
00:01:14,760 --> 00:01:17,830
Sixty-six years later, people walked on the Moon.

29
00:01:18,211 --> 00:01:20,435
Artificial intelligence spent decades on the

30
00:01:20,405 --> 00:01:21,920
flat part of that curve.

31
00:01:22,237 --> 00:01:25,936
Chess programs. Spam filters. Navigation apps that

32
00:01:25,906 --> 00:01:28,444
find the fastest route. Recommendations that

33
00:01:28,414 --> 00:01:30,567
know what you want to watch next.

34
00:01:30,892 --> 00:01:34,185
In 1997, IBM's Deep Blue beat the world

35
00:01:34,155 --> 00:01:37,517
chess champion. In 2016, AlphaGo beat one

36
00:01:37,487 --> 00:01:40,235
of the best Go players alive. Impressive,

37
00:01:40,205 --> 00:01:43,008
but each system could do exactly one thing.

38
00:01:43,502 --> 00:01:47,398
Then, in November 2022, ChatGPT arrived.

39
00:01:47,368 --> 00:01:48,931
Within about two months it reportedly

40
00:01:48,901 --> 00:01:51,172
passed one hundred million users, one of

41
00:01:51,142 --> 00:01:53,469
the fastest growing apps ever recorded.

42
00:01:53,821 --> 00:01:55,534
That was the moment the curve bent

43
00:01:55,504 --> 00:01:57,866
upward. And it has not stopped bending.

44
00:01:58,251 --> 00:02:01,486
Start with images. In 2022, AI

45
00:02:01,456 --> 00:02:04,133
pictures were a curiosity: melted faces,

46
00:02:04,103 --> 00:02:06,363
hands with seven fingers, and text that

47
00:02:06,333 --> 00:02:07,963
looked like a dream of text.

48
00:02:08,694 --> 00:02:10,791
Within two years, the best image

49
00:02:10,761 --> 00:02:12,823
generators were producing pictures most people

50
00:02:12,793 --> 00:02:15,192
could not tell from photographs, with correct

51
00:02:15,162 --> 00:02:19,219
reflections, believable skin and readable signs.

52
00:02:19,563 --> 00:02:21,543
That is a gift for designers and

53
00:02:21,513 --> 00:02:25,340
small businesses. It is also a problem.

54
00:02:25,310 --> 00:02:27,373
When a convincing image costs nothing and

55
00:02:27,343 --> 00:02:30,424
takes seconds, seeing is no longer believing.

56
00:02:30,919 --> 00:02:32,771
Standards like content credentials try to

57
00:02:32,741 --> 00:02:34,652
label how a file was made, but

58
00:02:34,622 --> 00:02:36,905
adoption is still patchy, and the fakes

59
00:02:36,875 --> 00:02:38,865
are improving faster than the labels.

60
00:02:39,278 --> 00:02:40,981
Video was supposed to be the hard

61
00:02:40,951 --> 00:02:44,011
one. In 2023 the internet laughed at

62
00:02:43,981 --> 00:02:45,950
a clip of a famous actor eating spaghetti

63
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:48,783
that looked like a nightmare. Faces melted.

64
00:02:48,753 --> 00:02:50,568
Noodles had a mind of their own.

65
00:02:50,958 --> 00:02:53,159
Two years later, new models were generating

66
00:02:53,129 --> 00:02:55,819
clips with realistic physics, smooth camera moves

67
00:02:55,789 --> 00:02:58,466
and, for the first time, synchronized voices and

68
00:02:58,436 --> 00:03:01,564
sound effects, all from a single sentence.

69
00:03:01,855 --> 00:03:03,453
Today a short film can be drafted

70
00:03:03,423 --> 00:03:05,641
in an afternoon. One person with a

71
00:03:05,611 --> 00:03:08,091
laptop can prototype scenes that once needed

72
00:03:08,061 --> 00:03:10,656
a crew, a location and a budget.

73
00:03:11,022 --> 00:03:14,164
The tools also changed existing footage: swapping

74
00:03:14,134 --> 00:03:16,230
a character into a scene, fixing where

75
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:18,645
an actor looks, turning a few photos into

76
00:03:18,615 --> 00:03:20,663
a 3D space you can walk through.

77
00:03:20,981 --> 00:03:23,601
The flip side is obvious. Anyone can now

78
00:03:23,571 --> 00:03:26,364
fabricate a video of anyone saying anything, which

79
00:03:26,334 --> 00:03:28,338
is why platforms ask creators to label

80
00:03:28,308 --> 00:03:31,122
AI-made scenes, including a few in this video.

81
00:03:31,522 --> 00:03:33,665
Voices are next. A few seconds of

82
00:03:33,635 --> 00:03:35,593
recording is now enough to clone someone's

83
00:03:35,563 --> 00:03:37,600
voice well enough to fool a friend.

84
00:03:37,928 --> 00:03:40,420
That makes dubbing a film into thirty languages,

85
00:03:40,390 --> 00:03:43,427
in the actor's own voice, almost routine. It

86
00:03:43,397 --> 00:03:45,830
also powers a new kind of scam: a

87
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:48,139
phone call from your daughter asking for money.

88
00:03:48,482 --> 00:03:51,079
A simple defense: agree on a family

89
00:03:51,049 --> 00:03:53,631
code word that only real relatives know.

90
00:03:54,129 --> 00:03:56,969
Music followed. Type a sentence, get a

91
00:03:56,939 --> 00:03:58,850
full song with vocals. For a

92
00:03:58,820 --> 00:04:01,785
while, the record labels answered with lawsuits.

93
00:04:02,118 --> 00:04:06,535
Then the mood changed. In late 2025, Warner

94
00:04:06,505 --> 00:04:09,693
settled with Suno and Udio, and Universal

95
00:04:09,663 --> 00:04:12,316
settled with Udio. The fight moved from

96
00:04:12,286 --> 00:04:15,681
'stop' to 'license it, and share the money.'

97
00:04:16,016 --> 00:04:18,543
Whether artists really benefit from those deals

98
00:04:18,513 --> 00:04:20,447
is still being argued. But the

99
00:04:20,417 --> 00:04:23,835
era of ignoring AI music is over.

100
00:04:24,283 --> 00:04:26,287
The most important change, though, was

101
00:04:26,257 --> 00:04:27,517
not a picture or a

102
00:04:27,487 --> 00:04:29,849
song. It was a chat box.

103
00:04:30,202 --> 00:04:32,172
You write in plain language, and

104
00:04:32,142 --> 00:04:35,980
it answers: it explains tax letters, fixes

105
00:04:35,950 --> 00:04:39,544
a spreadsheet formula, drafts an email, helps

106
00:04:39,514 --> 00:04:42,143
you learn a language, writes code.

107
00:04:42,451 --> 00:04:45,176
Assistants now sit inside the tools people already

108
00:04:45,146 --> 00:04:51,513
use: documents, spreadsheets, search, phones and programming editors.

109
00:04:51,826 --> 00:04:54,073
And they are changing shape. At

110
00:04:54,043 --> 00:04:57,996
first they only talked. Now they act.

111
00:04:58,421 --> 00:05:00,193
An agent is an assistant that can use

112
00:05:00,163 --> 00:05:04,025
tools: browse the web, open files, run

113
00:05:03,995 --> 00:05:07,438
code, fill in forms, and keep going until

114
00:05:07,408 --> 00:05:10,155
the task is done. The first experiments

115
00:05:10,125 --> 00:05:14,704
in 2023, like AutoGPT, looped and got lost.

116
00:05:15,083 --> 00:05:17,226
Now agents can work on programming tasks

117
00:05:17,196 --> 00:05:19,413
for hours at a time. One research

118
00:05:19,383 --> 00:05:22,571
group, METR, has measured that the length

119
00:05:22,541 --> 00:05:24,463
of tasks AI can finish on its

120
00:05:24,433 --> 00:05:27,213
own has been doubling every few months.

121
00:05:27,606 --> 00:05:29,552
That does not make them reliable.

122
00:05:29,522 --> 00:05:32,014
They still make confident mistakes, which

123
00:05:31,984 --> 00:05:33,825
is why the best results come from

124
00:05:33,795 --> 00:05:35,681
a human who checks the work.

125
00:05:36,066 --> 00:05:37,571
Which brings us to the question

126
00:05:37,541 --> 00:05:40,518
everyone asks: what happens to jobs?

127
00:05:40,931 --> 00:05:43,306
So far the picture is mixed, but one

128
00:05:43,276 --> 00:05:47,556
pattern keeps showing up. Stanford economists studying payroll

129
00:05:47,526 --> 00:05:49,565
data found that young workers in the

130
00:05:49,535 --> 00:05:53,768
most AI-exposed jobs lost ground, mostly because companies

131
00:05:53,738 --> 00:05:57,237
hired fewer beginners, not because they fired people.

132
00:05:57,474 --> 00:06:01,104
That matters, because entry-level work is where careers

133
00:06:01,074 --> 00:06:03,507
start. If the simple tasks go to

134
00:06:03,477 --> 00:06:06,930
software, how does anyone learn the hard ones?

135
00:06:07,267 --> 00:06:11,952
History has examples. Telephone switchboards, typesetting and film

136
00:06:11,922 --> 00:06:15,041
developing once employed huge numbers of people.

137
00:06:15,011 --> 00:06:17,259
New jobs appeared, but not for the

138
00:06:17,229 --> 00:06:20,485
same people, and not at the same time.

139
00:06:20,750 --> 00:06:23,578
The honest answer is that nobody knows the

140
00:06:23,548 --> 00:06:26,338
final number. What we do know is that

141
00:06:26,308 --> 00:06:31,086
the valuable skills are shifting: judgment, taste, knowing

142
00:06:31,056 --> 00:06:34,161
what to ask, and checking what comes back.

143
00:06:34,690 --> 00:06:37,124
Now the big question. Today's systems

144
00:06:37,094 --> 00:06:39,946
are narrow in a strange way: brilliant

145
00:06:39,916 --> 00:06:42,870
at some tasks, clueless at others.

146
00:06:43,283 --> 00:06:47,028
Artificial general intelligence, or AGI, means a system

147
00:06:46,998 --> 00:06:49,304
that can learn and reason across almost any

148
00:06:49,274 --> 00:06:52,286
intellectual task as well as a capable human.

149
00:06:52,615 --> 00:06:56,198
When could that happen? Experts disagree wildly.

150
00:06:56,168 --> 00:06:57,743
Some lab leaders say within a few

151
00:06:57,713 --> 00:07:00,912
years. A large 2023 survey of researchers

152
00:07:00,882 --> 00:07:03,745
put the median guess around 2047, and

153
00:07:03,715 --> 00:07:06,590
many forecasts have moved earlier since. Others

154
00:07:06,560 --> 00:07:08,563
doubt today's approach gets there at all.

155
00:07:08,949 --> 00:07:11,753
Nobody knows. But the people building these systems

156
00:07:11,723 --> 00:07:14,366
agree on one thing: if it happens,

157
00:07:14,336 --> 00:07:16,767
it will not be like another app update.

158
00:07:17,064 --> 00:07:19,660
It could speed up science, medicine and

159
00:07:19,630 --> 00:07:23,504
clean energy. It could also concentrate power, and

160
00:07:23,474 --> 00:07:26,253
in the wrong hands, cause real harm.

161
00:07:26,610 --> 00:07:29,114
That is why governments are writing rules.

162
00:07:29,084 --> 00:07:31,146
Europe's AI Act has been phasing in

163
00:07:31,116 --> 00:07:33,956
since 2024, and labs now run

164
00:07:33,926 --> 00:07:37,217
safety tests before releasing their strongest models.

165
00:07:37,634 --> 00:07:39,859
Meanwhile, there is something you can do

166
00:07:39,829 --> 00:07:42,877
today: use these tools, learn what they are

167
00:07:42,847 --> 00:07:45,209
good at, and notice where they fail.

168
00:07:45,553 --> 00:07:47,570
And to stay current, follow a

169
00:07:47,540 --> 00:07:50,344
few good sources, and be skeptical

170
00:07:50,314 --> 00:07:52,955
of anyone who sounds completely certain.

171
00:07:53,368 --> 00:07:55,906
The curve is steep right now.

172
00:07:55,876 --> 00:07:58,332
The real question is not whether AI

173
00:07:58,302 --> 00:08:00,782
changes everything. It is who gets to

174
00:08:00,752 --> 00:08:03,195
decide where the curve levels off.

175
00:08:03,553 --> 00:08:05,557
Tell us in the comments: what

176
00:08:05,527 --> 00:08:06,869
is the first thing you

177
00:08:06,839 --> 00:08:08,783
would hand over to an AI?

